---
/**
 * How to hold any of the four portfolios: what to do before investing, which account
 * each fund goes in (with the calculator), how to buy, when to rebalance, what to do
 * when it falls, what to do if RSST closes, and the broker fee to check for.
 *
 * Every number that describes what a portfolio did is read from the research emitter's
 * series at build time. The worked purchase example is the Plus trend weights on $10,000.
 */
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import PlacementTool from "~/components/islands/PlacementTool";
import { PORTFOLIOS, WITH_TREND_HOLDINGS } from "~/content/portfolios";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import { formatDollars, formatPct } from "~/lib/charts";
import { loadSeries, seriesFor } from "~/lib/series";

const file = loadSeries("1990");
const market = seriesFor("market");

/** What each portfolio, and the plain index, did in 2008 and 2022, and its worst fall. */
const FALLS = [...PORTFOLIOS.map((p) => seriesFor(p.slug)), market].map((s) => {
  const y2008 = s.summary.calendarYears["2008"];
  const y2022 = s.summary.calendarYears["2022"];
  if (y2008 === undefined || y2022 === undefined) throw new Error(`${s.id} is missing a calendar year`);
  const months = s.summary.worstFall.monthsToRecover;
  return {
    id: s.id,
    label: s.id === "market" ? "A plain world stock index" : s.label,
    y2008,
    y2022,
    y2008Text: formatPct(y2008),
    y2022Text: formatPct(y2022),
    trough: formatDollars(s.summary.worstFall.dollarsAtTrough),
    months: months === null ? "not yet" : String(months),
  };
});

const plain = FALLS[FALLS.length - 1];
if (plain === undefined) throw new Error("no series to print");
const four = FALLS.filter((row) => row.id !== "market");
/** The smallest and largest fall among the four portfolios in a calendar year, as positive percents. */
function range(year: "y2008" | "y2022"): [string, string] {
  const falls = four.map((row) => -row[year]);
  return [formatPct(Math.min(...falls)), formatPct(Math.max(...falls))];
}
const [least2008, most2008] = range("y2008");
const [least2022, most2022] = range("y2022");

/** The Plus trend weights as dollars of a $10,000 purchase. */
const EXAMPLE = WITH_TREND_HOLDINGS.map((h) => `${formatDollars(h.weight * 100, "exact")} of ${h.ticker}`);
const exampleText = `${EXAMPLE.slice(0, -1).join(", ")} and ${EXAMPLE[EXAMPLE.length - 1]}`;

---

<Base
  title="How to hold it"
  description="Which account each fund goes in, how to buy, when to rebalance, what to do when the portfolio falls, and what to do if RSST closes."
>
  <Hero
    title="How to hold it"
    lede="Choose an allocation you can hold, put it into your actual accounts, and use a written review routine."
  />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>Everything here about tax is US federal tax. State tax adds on top, and outside the US every line
    differs.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="before-you-invest">Before you invest</h2>

    <p>Keep money for near-term spending and emergencies outside an allocation that can fall sharply.
    Compare paying down expensive debt with investing, and check any employer retirement match and its
    vesting rules before deciding where new money goes.</p>

    <p>A health savings account can be useful if you qualify, but a high deductible alone does not establish
    eligibility. Other coverage and Medicare matter too. Check the
    <a href="https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8889">IRS eligibility and contribution rules</a>
    before contributing.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="which-account">Which account</h2>

    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>If all your money is in one account, skip this section. Buy the funds in the weights shown and go to
      <a href="#rebalancing">Rebalancing</a>.</p>
    </Callout>

    <p>Set one allocation across your accounts together. You do not need to hold every fund in every account.</p>

    <p>A retirement account can defer or avoid tax on a fund's annual distributions, making it a useful home for taxable
    bond income or some trend strategies. Broad stock funds are often easier to hold in a taxable account.
    Foreign funds add a tradeoff: avoiding US tax on their dividends can forfeit a foreign tax credit.</p>

    <p>The order depends on your tax rates, available space, fund distributions and eventual withdrawals.
    Roth and pre-tax balances are not interchangeable after tax. The calculator compares the Plus trend
    holdings under your inputs; it does not assume that everyone should use the same account order.</p>

    <p>Nothing you enter leaves your browser.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <PlacementTool client:visible />
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-10">
    <p>If an employer plan does not offer these tickers, compare its broad funds by exposure and cost.
    A rollover is a separate decision: compare total fees, investment choices, withdrawal rules and account
    protections before moving retirement money. A larger fund menu alone does not establish a better account.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="how-to-buy">How to buy</h2>

    <p>Check that your broker offers each fund and inspect its order-preview fees. Buy by ticker in the
    proportions you chose. As a worked example, $10,000 in Plus trend is
    <span data-numeric>{exampleText}</span>. Direct new money toward holdings below their targets,
    within the contribution limits and access rules of the accounts you use.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="rebalancing">Rebalancing</h2>

    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>Once a year, compare the holdings across all your accounts with your targets. Use contributions,
      withdrawals and cash distributions to bring them closer. Where possible, make additional trades inside
      retirement accounts. If taxable sales are needed, compare the tax and trading cost with the risk of
      keeping the current allocation.</p>
    </Callout>

    <p>Rebalancing controls exposure; it does not promise extra return. If a taxable holding alone exceeds
    its whole-portfolio target, trades in other accounts cannot fix that. More contributions, a revised target
    or a taxable sale may be appropriate. The SEC's
    <a href="https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/general-resources/publications-research/info-sheets/beginners-guide-asset">rebalancing guide</a>
    describes these methods and their tax and fee tradeoffs.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="when-it-falls">When it falls</h2>

    <p>Sitting through a fall looks like this. In 2008 the four portfolios fell between
    <span data-numeric>{least2008}</span> and <span data-numeric>{most2008}</span> in one year, and in 2022
    between <span data-numeric>{least2022}</span> and <span data-numeric>{most2022}</span>. At the worst point,
    $10,000 in the plain world stock index was worth <span data-numeric>{plain.trough}</span> and took
    <span data-numeric>{plain.months}</span> months to get back to $10,000. Every number is a simulation from
    index data, before tax, over {file.window.label}.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6 max-w-measure overflow-x-auto" tabindex="0" aria-label="What each portfolio did in 2008 and 2022">
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm sm:text-base">
      <caption class="sr-only">Each portfolio's return in calendar 2008 and 2022, what $10,000 was worth at the bottom of its worst fall, and the months it took to recover, {file.window.label}, simulated</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left">
          <th scope="col" class="label py-2 pr-2 font-semibold">Portfolio</th>
          <th scope="col" class="label py-2 pr-2 text-right font-semibold">2008</th>
          <th scope="col" class="label py-2 pr-2 text-right font-semibold">2022</th>
          <th scope="col" class="label py-2 pr-2 text-right font-semibold">$10,000 at the bottom</th>
          <th scope="col" class="label py-2 text-right font-semibold">Months back to even</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-ink-2">
        {FALLS.map((row) => (
          <tr class="border-b border-rule last:border-0">
            <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-2 text-left font-medium text-ink">{row.label}</th>
            <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap">{row.y2008Text}</td>
            <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap">{row.y2022Text}</td>
            <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap">{row.trough}</td>
            <td data-numeric class="py-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap">{row.months}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-8">
    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>Before investing, write down the losses and time behind a benchmark you can accept. A market fall
      alone is not a reason to abandon the plan. A change in your spending needs, income, risk tolerance or
      the fund itself is a reason to review it.</p>
    </Callout>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="if-rsst-closes">If RSST closes</h2>

    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>Read the fund's closure or mandate-change notice and review your replacement before its deadline.
      Moving RSST into VTI would remove the trend holding while retaining broad US stock exposure. It leaves
      the rest of your portfolio, including its bond holding, in place.</p>
    </Callout>

    <p>A standalone trend fund uses capital that would otherwise hold stocks or bonds; it does not reproduce
    RSST's stacked exposures automatically. Compare the whole resulting allocation, fees and tax effects.
    Trading within a retirement account generally does not create an immediate capital-gains tax, but account
    withdrawal rules still apply. The <a href="/portfolios/with-trend/">Plus trend page</a> describes the
    fund's risks.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="broker-fees">Broker fees</h2>

    <p>A broker can charge a purchase fee even when the ETF itself has a low annual fee. Check the exact
    ticker, account and order type before buying; charges can change. Include commissions, bid/ask spreads
    and account charges when comparing brokers.</p>

    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>If an order carries a large fee, compare another broker or an appropriate fund with similar exposure.
      Larger, less frequent purchases can reduce a fixed charge, but delaying investment also has a cost.
      Use the actual fee schedule rather than assuming one trading routine fits every charge.</p>
    </Callout>
    <p>The SEC's <a href="https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins/updated">guide to fees and expenses</a>
    explains what to look for.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
